<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enver Hoxha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1908">1908</a>–<a href="/years/1985">1985</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enver Hoxha was the communist ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> from 1944 until his death in 1985. A founder and first secretary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/party-of-labour-of-albania">Albanian Communist Party</a> in 1941, he led the Partisans to victory in 1944 and built a Stalinist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>. Breaking in turn with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, he left Albania deeply isolated, and in 1967 he declared it the world's first atheist state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha.jpg" alt="Enver Hoxha" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petrit Kumi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enver_Hoxha_portrait_(colour_correction).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Enver Hoxha was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjirokaster">Gjirokastër</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> and then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, in October 1908. His father, Halil Hoxha, was a Shia Muslim cloth merchant who travelled widely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and the family belonged to the Bektashi Sufi order. He was named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-pasha">Enver Pasha</a>, a leading figure of the Young Turk Revolution. He studied at the Gjirokastër Lyceum from 1923, and when it closed he received a state scholarship to continue at the Albanian National Lyceum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korce">Korçë</a>, where he studied until 1930.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/party-of-labour-of-albania">Communist Party of Albania</a> was founded on 8 November 1941, and the two Yugoslav envoys chose Hoxha from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korce">Korça</a> group as a Muslim representative among the seven members of its provisional Central Committee. The Albanian Communists repudiated a Greater Albania agreement when the Yugoslav Communists disagreed with it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balli-kombetar">Balli Kombëtar</a>, which condemned them, then sided with Nazi Germany, leaving the Communists in the lead of the fight for liberation.</p><p>In power from 1944, Hoxha admired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>. Between 1945 and 1950 the government consolidated power through policies that included extrajudicial killings of anti-communists. The Agrarian Reform Law of August 1945 took land from beys and large landowners without compensation, cutting their share from 52 per cent of the land to 16 per cent. In February 1951, after a bombing at the Soviet embassy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a>, 23 intellectuals were arrested; one was killed during interrogation and the other 22 were executed without trial under Hoxha's orders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comecon">Comecon</a> on 22 February 1949 after the break with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. When Stalin died in March 1953, Hoxha had the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a> kneel in the main square and swear an oath of eternal fidelity. Aid fell under Khrushchev, and Albania later turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, then severed ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> after the Sino-Albanian Split of 1976 to 1978. In 1966 he led the Cultural and Ideological Revolution, which sought to remove the influence of religion; the Jesuit Order had been banned in 1946 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> in 1947.</p><p>After 1968 travel abroad was forbidden to all but those on official business, and at one point every third Albanian had been questioned by the Sigurimi or held in a labour camp. Hoxha died on 11 April 1985 and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiz-alia">Ramiz Alia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Enver Hoxha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>China and the Soviet Union were at sword’s point by 1960, splitting the world movement into two hostile camps, and anti-revisionists such as the Maoists and <b>Enver Hoxha</b>’s followers rejected the Soviet line after Khrushchev.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>From within Marxism–Leninism, <b>Enver Hoxha</b> of Albania denounced Deng as a revisionist in 1978 and attacked Maoism itself, arguing that New Democracy halts class struggle, that the Three Worlds Theory is “counter-revolutionary”, and questioning Mao’s guerrilla methods.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Albania's assembly abolished the monarchy and declared a People's Republic under communist leader <b>Enver Hoxha</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-11">11 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Resistance grew, including <b>Enver Hoxha's</b> Communist Party, founded in 1941.</blockquote><a href="/state/albanian-kingdom-1939-1943">Albanian Kingdom (1939-1943)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Enver Hoxha's</b> Partisans won the civil war and took Tirana on 17 November 1944 as German forces withdrew.</blockquote><a href="/state/albanian-kingdom-1943-1944">Albanian Kingdom (1943-1944)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Enver Hoxha's</b> Communist-led Partisans took power as German forces withdrew in November 1944 from the Albanian Kingdom (1943-1944).</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-socialist-republic-of-albania">People's Socialist Republic of Albania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjirokaster">Gjirokastër</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.08,20.14&amp;year=1958" aria-label="Gjirokastër on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5046 730 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Enver Hoxha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5496 1000h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5496 1000h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.08,20.14&amp;year=1958">What was here in 1958? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
